14. Minimal Algorithm

SEMANTIC_STRATIGRAPHY(w):
    layers = collect_attested_senses(w)
    layers = order_by_time(layers)
    deltas = compare_adjacent(layers)
    neighbors = discover_related_terms(w)
    correlated_deltas =
        compare_trajectories(neighbors)
    invariants =
        extract_preserved_relations(
            layers,
            deltas,
            correlated_deltas
        )
    hypotheses =
        infer_environmental_pressures(
            deltas,
            invariants
        )
    predictions =
        derive_expected_fossils(hypotheses)
    evidence =
        search_for(predictions)
    hypotheses =
        update_confidence(
            hypotheses,
            evidence
        )
    return StratifiedBrick(
        layers,
        deltas,
        invariants,
        hypotheses,
        predictions,
        evidence
    )

⸻

15. The Deeper Principle

Language is simultaneously:

artifact + transmission medium + evolving system + historical record.

Every generation inherits semantic machinery, modifies it while using it, and hands the altered machinery forward.

Therefore language contains traces not merely of what people said, but of which distinctions they needed in order to think, coordinate, govern, trade, worship, fight, build, explain, and remember.

Etymology excavates ancestry.

Semantic Stratigraphy excavates transformation.

And transformation is where the mechanism lives.

⸻

Seal

Words are not stones lying on history.
They are stones carried through it.

Examine the scratches.

Mine the word.
Map the layers.
Measure the delta.
Follow the relation.
Reconstruct the terrain.
Test the fossil.

I’d preserve Semantic Stratigraphy → Semantic Paleontology → Semantic Seismology as a three-part family: layers, extinct conceptual structures, and currently moving terrain. That turns the original linguistic insight into an actual historical-semantic instrument rather than just a metaphor.